    <!-- 6 STANDARD ENGLISH -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Standard English</span>
      <h2>Using spoken Standard English</h2>
      <p>A key focus is <b>Standard English</b> — the formal variety expected in presentations. It is about <b>register</b>, not accent:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Standard grammar</b> — "we were", not "we was"; "I did it", not "I done it".</li>
        <li><b>Full words</b> — "going to", not "gonna"; avoid heavy slang and filler ("like", "you know").</li>
        <li><b>Precise vocabulary</b> — choose accurate, varied words for a formal setting.</li>
        <li><b>Clear delivery</b> — you can keep your own accent; Standard English is about the words and grammar you choose.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Accent ≠ Standard English.</b> You are never penalised for your accent — only for informal grammar or vocabulary that doesn't fit a formal presentation.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 Q: Standard English MCQ -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Spot the Standard English</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>Which sentence uses <b>spoken Standard English</b> suitable for a formal presentation?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q3" data-why="'My team and I were determined to succeed' uses standard grammar and formal vocabulary. 'Me and my mates was gonna…' mixes non-standard grammar ('was'), casual pronouns and contractions ('gonna') that don't fit a formal talk.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">"Me and my mates was gonna do it, innit." <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">"My team and I were determined to succeed." <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">"We done loads of stuff, like, and it was well good." <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">"So yeah, basically we just kinda did the thing." <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q3"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 DELIVERY -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Delivery skill</span>
      <h2>Delivering with confidence</h2>
      <p>How you say it matters as much as what you say. Strong delivery:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Eye contact</b> — look up and around the room, not at your cards.</li>
        <li><b>Pace &amp; pauses</b> — slow down; a pause lets a key point land.</li>
        <li><b>Volume &amp; clarity</b> — project so the back row hears every word.</li>
        <li><b>Body language</b> — stand tall, use open gestures, avoid fidgeting.</li>
        <li><b>Tone</b> — vary it to keep the audience engaged.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Nerves are normal.</b> Rehearse aloud several times — familiarity is the best cure for shaky delivery.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 9 RHETORIC IN SPEECH -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Persuasive toolkit</span>
      <h2>Rhetoric to engage listeners</h2>
      <p>Great speakers weave in rhetorical devices — but each must do a <b>job</b>, not just tick a box:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Direct address ("you")</b> — draws each listener in personally.</li>
        <li><b>Rhetorical question</b> — makes the audience think and agree.</li>
        <li><b>Triples (rule of three)</b> — memorable, rhythmic emphasis.</li>
        <li><b>Anecdote</b> — a short story that makes the point human.</li>
        <li><b>Repetition</b> — hammers home a key phrase.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Use a few well.</b> One vivid anecdote and a well-placed rhetorical question beat a scattergun of every device at once.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 Q: rhetoric MCQ -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Name the device</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>A speaker ends: "We can act now, we can act together, we can act for the future." Which device is this?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q4" data-why="'We can act now, we can act together, we can act for the future' repeats a structure three times — a triple (rule of three), which also uses repetition for rhythm and emphasis, making the ending memorable.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">A rhetorical question <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">A triple (rule of three) with repetition <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">An anecdote <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">A statistic <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q4"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 11 GAME 1 classify device->effect -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Mini-game</span>
      <h2>Device → effect on the audience</h2>
      <p style="font-size:1.02rem;color:var(--soft)">Tap the <b>effect</b> that best matches each spoken-language device.</p>
      <div class="activity">
        <div id="classify"></div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-cl"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 12 LISTENING & RESPONDING -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Respond to questions</span>
      <h2>Listening and responding</h2>
      <p>After your talk, the audience asks questions. This part assesses whether you can <b>listen and respond appropriately</b>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Listen fully</b> — don't start answering before the question is finished.</li>
        <li><b>Answer the actual question</b> — address what was asked, not what you wish had been asked.</li>
        <li><b>Develop your answer</b> — give a reason or example, not just "yes".</li>
        <li><b>Stay composed</b> — it's fine to think for a moment, or to acknowledge a good point.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>This is a two-way skill.</b> Responding well to a tricky question shows more than a polished script — it shows you can think on your feet.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 13 Q: responding MCQ -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Best response</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>An audience member asks: "But wouldn't your idea cost too much?" What is the strongest way to respond?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q5" data-why="The best response listens, acknowledges the point, and answers it directly with a developed reason. Ignoring it, dismissing it, or waffling off-topic all fail the 'listen and respond appropriately' focus.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Ignore it and repeat your original point <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">"That's a fair point — it would cost more at first, but I'd argue the long-term savings outweigh it, because…" <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">"I don't know, next question." <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Change the subject to something unrelated <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q5"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 14 COMMON PITFALLS -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Avoid these</span>
      <h2>Common pitfalls</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Reading a script word-for-word</b> — kills eye contact and sounds flat.</li>
        <li><b>Rushing</b> — nerves speed you up; consciously slow down.</li>
        <li><b>Slipping into slang or filler</b> — undermines the Standard English focus.</li>
        <li><b>No clear point</b> — a talk that drifts loses the audience.</li>
        <li><b>Freezing on questions</b> — prepare for likely questions in advance.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Rehearse in front of someone.</b> A trial run with real questions is the single best preparation.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 15 GAME 2 match preparation step -> reason -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Mini-game</span>
      <h2>Match tip → why it helps</h2>
      <p style="font-size:1.02rem;color:var(--soft)">Tap a <b>preparation tip</b> on the left, then the <b>reason</b> it helps on the right.</p>
      <div class="activity">
        <div id="matchGame"></div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-match"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 16 WORKED MODEL OPENING -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Worked model</span>
      <h2>A strong opening, labelled</h2>
      <p>A short model opening for a presentation on why libraries matter, with the techniques marked:</p>
      <div class="extract">
        "Imagine a town with no quiet place to think, no free books, no door open to everyone. <b>That</b> is the town we are building, one closure at a time. Today I want to persuade you that our library is worth fighting for — for our children, our elderly, and ourselves."
        <span class="an">▸ rhetorical image / direct address ("you") · repetition ("no… no… no…") · triple ("our children, our elderly, and ourselves")</span>
      </div>
      <p class="note">The opening hooks the audience, states a clear purpose, and signals the structure to come.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 17 Q: pitfall MCQ -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>What went wrong?</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>A student reads their whole talk word-for-word from a full script, eyes down, in a flat monotone. Which skill area suffers most?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q6" data-why="Reading a full script with no eye contact and a flat tone directly weakens delivery and audience engagement. Cue cards, eye contact and varied tone are what a strong presentation needs.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Their spelling <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Delivery and engagement — no eye contact, no vocal variety <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Their choice of topic <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">The length of their script <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q6"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 18 REGISTER FOR AUDIENCE -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Audience awareness</span>
      <h2>Judging your audience</h2>
      <p>Adapt your talk to who is listening while keeping it <b>formal</b>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Peers</b> — a warmer, punchier tone; still Standard English, no slang.</li>
        <li><b>Teachers / adults</b> — a more measured, respectful register.</li>
        <li><b>A mixed audience</b> — pitch it so everyone can follow; explain any jargon.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Formal doesn't mean dull.</b> You can be lively and personal while still using Standard English and a clear structure.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 19 Q: register MCQ -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Reading the room</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>You're presenting to your class and teacher. Which approach best fits a <b>formal presentation</b> to that audience?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q7" data-why="A lively but Standard-English, well-structured talk suits a formal presentation to peers and a teacher. Pure slang is too informal; a monotone essay read aloud is dull; going off on unrelated tangents loses the audience.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Full slang and in-jokes throughout <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Lively and engaging, but in clear Standard English with a strong structure <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Read a dense essay in a flat monotone <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Wander off on unrelated tangents to fill time <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q7"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 20 GAME 3 sort behaviour -> helps/hurts -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Mini-game</span>
      <h2>Sort: helps or hurts your grade?</h2>
      <p style="font-size:1.02rem;color:var(--soft)">Tap a behaviour, then the box for whether it <b>helps</b> or <b>hurts</b> your presentation.</p>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="chips" id="resChips"></div>
        <div class="bins">
          <div class="bin ren"><h3>✅ Helps</h3><div class="drop" id="binRen"></div></div>
          <div class="bin non"><h3>⚠️ Hurts</h3><div class="drop" id="binNon"></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-res"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 21 Q: purpose of endorsement MCQ -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Listen, then respond</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>Which pair of skills does the <b>question-and-answer</b> part of the endorsement mainly assess?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q8" data-why="The Q&A tests your ability to LISTEN to a question and RESPOND appropriately — a two-way skill. It isn't about spelling or a memorised script; it's about handling unplanned questions with composure and relevance.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Spelling and punctuation <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Listening carefully and responding appropriately <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Reciting your script from memory <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Reading aloud at speed <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q8"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 22 Q: overall MCQ -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Aiming for Distinction</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>What best describes a <b>Distinction</b>-level presentation?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q9" data-why="A top presentation combines a well-structured, engaging talk in confident Standard English WITH assured, relevant responses to questions. A polished script alone isn't enough if the speaker can't handle the Q&A or engage the audience.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">The longest talk in the class <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">A confident, well-structured talk in Standard English, plus assured, relevant answers to questions <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">A memorised script read without pausing <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">The most slides and animations <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q9"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 23 RECAP -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Recap</span>
      <h2>The skills to take into your talk</h2>
      <div class="recap">
        <p><b>Prepare:</b> clear purpose, one main line of argument, cue cards not a script.</p>
        <p><b>Structure:</b> hook → signposted points with evidence → strong close.</p>
        <p><b>Standard English:</b> formal grammar and vocabulary (accent is fine).</p>
        <p><b>Deliver:</b> eye contact, pace, pauses, projection, varied tone.</p>
        <p><b>Respond:</b> listen fully, answer the actual question, develop your answer.</p>
      </div>
      <p>You've trained every core skill the Edexcel Spoken Language Endorsement tests. Press <b>Finish</b> to see your score.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 24 FINAL -->
    <section class="screen final">
      <div class="trophy">🏆</div>
      <h1>Mini-lesson complete!</h1>
      <div class="stars3" id="finalStars">⭐⭐⭐</div>
      <p class="big">You've worked through the <b>Edexcel Spoken Language Endorsement</b>. 🎉</p>
      <p>Your stars: <span class="scorebig" id="finalScore">0</span> / <span id="finalMax">0</span></p>
      <p style="font-size:1.02rem;color:var(--soft)">Next: test yourself in the <b>Evaluate</b> stage Confidence Quiz, then lock it in with <b>Verify</b>.</p>
      <div class="sharebox">
        <h2>📣 Smashed it? Share your score</h2>
        <p style="font-size:1rem;color:var(--soft);margin-bottom:0">Challenge a mate to beat your stars, or show a parent how you got on.</p>
        <div class="sharerow">
          <button type="button" class="btn" data-share="mate">📲 Challenge a mate</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn ghost" data-share="parent">👪 Show your parents</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <button class="btn restart" onclick="location.reload()" style="margin-top:14px">Restart 🔄</button>
      <p style="margin-top:14px"><a href="../../subjects.html" style="color:var(--kinetic);font-weight:700;text-decoration:none">→ Back to all subjects</a></p>
    </section>

  </div>
